※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4474

※ A function that answers with something is a function whose answer is the
※ point of calling it, so by default a call whose answer goes nowhere is either
※ a mistake or a leftover -- and in a language emitted by a generator a leftover
※ is a defect in the generator, which is why this is an error rather than a
※ warning.
※
※ The two ways of saying that the answer really is not wanted are one line and
※ one word: `_ ← f()` for this call, and `@[can_ignore]` on the function where
※ it is true of every call to it.

fn twice(n: u8) → u8:
    n + n

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    twice(4u8)
    0u8
